+# Decision: How concurrent edits to one visit become a current record
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same visit independently — often after comparing notes in the field — and their edits can arrive days apart on tablets that have been offline. The record store must turn two independently edited copies of one visit into a single current record that review and export can rely on, without silently losing either worker's observations and without the server pretending that whoever synced last is more correct.
+
+The hard part is that the server cannot tell, from field names alone, whether two edits are independent or scientifically entangled. Correcting an observer's name and adding a missing species are unrelated; revising a species identification and its cover estimate are one thought expressed in two fields. Both look identical to code. Whatever mechanism is chosen determines the submission envelope the tablets build, the shape of the work coordinators do, and which records are eligible to leave in the Friday export — so tablet-sync and review-export are sized around this decision.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option here: both preserve every original submission (the "do not silently discard a submitted edit" tenet is a foundational invariant both respect, not a discriminator). The decision turns on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Never silently combine scientifically related edits** *(major; turns on the non-goal "no automated inference of scientific intent")*. A mechanism that combines two edits into one record without a human looking risks fusing a changed identification with a stale cover estimate — a data-integrity fault that is invisible precisely because nothing flagged it.
+- **Inspectability of the submitted→exported path** *(major; turns on the tenet "keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable")*. For any exported value, a coordinator must be able to see which submission(s) it came from.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** *(moderate; turns on the tenet "prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator")*. Rules that need ongoing tending are a standing cost the team may not be able to carry.
+- **Review workload** *(moderate)*. How many visits reach export without a human touching them — the volume of items landing in the coordinators' queue.
+- **Reviewer effort per item** *(moderate)*. How much a coordinator must read to resolve one queued item. This cuts *against* the recommended option and was not surfaced in the original trade framing.
+- **Submission size / tablet storage** *(minor)*. What each tablet must retain and transmit per edit. At ~6,000 records/season across 12 tablets this is not binding, but it is not nothing.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission is a complete visit payload paired with the `baseRevisionId` it was edited from. The server stores it as an immutable revision and never combines fields. When two accepted revisions descend from the same base, they stand as *competing revisions*; the visit is unresolved until a coordinator picks a winner (or authors a reconciling revision), and that resolution records the coordinator and the contributing revision IDs. A submission whose base is the current revision simply extends the line.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and Worker B both fetch revision `r3` of a visit. A fixes an observer name and submits (based on `r3`); B adds a species and submits (based on `r3`). Both are retained; the visit is unresolved with two competing revisions; a coordinator sees both whole visits side by side and resolves.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- **Never silently combine** — *strong*. The server performs no combination at all; every combination is a coordinator's explicit act.
+- **Inspectability** — *strong*. The exported record is one named revision, traceable to its submission; losers remain in history.
+- **Implementation/maintenance burden** — *strong*. No per-field merge rules to write or maintain; the logic is base-pointer comparison and append.
+- **Review workload** — *weak*. Any fork creates a queued item, including edits to entirely unrelated fields that could in principle have coexisted.
+- **Reviewer effort per item** — *weak*. The coordinator compares whole visits even when a single field differs; the system does not narrow attention to what actually diverged.
+- **Submission size** — *partial*. Each submission carries the full payload plus a base ID; no base snapshot is retained on the tablet beyond the revision it holds.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger, coarser review queue for a server that never guesses.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each tablet retains the base snapshot it edited from and submits structured, field-level changes against it. The server performs a three-way merge: changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current record; only changes that touch the same field surface as conflicts, carrying field-level provenance (which submission supplied each field) and a review view scoped to the overlapping fields. Merge behavior is defined per field kind — scalars (date, notes), the observer list, and the species-cover entries each need their own rule for what "disjoint" and "conflict" mean (e.g. are two added species disjoint, or is the species set one field?).
+
+**Demonstration.** Same `r3` start: A's observer-name fix and B's added species touch different fields, so the server merges them into one record with no review. Had both edited the same species' cover estimate, only that estimate would surface as a conflict, showing A's value and B's value with their authors.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- **Never silently combine** — *weak*. Disjoint-by-field is not the same as unrelated-in-science; a merge rule can fuse a revised identification with an untouched-but-now-wrong cover estimate and report success. The failure is silent by construction.
+- **Inspectability** — *partial*. Field-level provenance makes each field traceable, but the exported record is a server-synthesized composite that no worker ever submitted or saw whole.
+- **Implementation/maintenance burden** — *weak*. Needs a maintained merge rule per field kind, and those rules carry the structural-vs-scientific ambiguity as ongoing correctness risk — exactly the kind of system a two-coordinator team has no administrator to tend.
+- **Review workload** — *strong*. Only genuine same-field conflicts queue; most independent corrections clear automatically, so more visits reach export untouched.
+- **Reviewer effort per item** — *strong*. A conflict is pre-narrowed to the overlapping fields, so each queued item is small and focused.
+- **Submission size** — *partial*. The tablet retains a base snapshot and transmits a structured diff rather than a whole payload.
+
+**Summary.** Trades maintained merge rules and composite records for a smaller, sharper review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the two major criteria, and they point the same way. On *never silently combine*, whole-visit revisions are strong and merge is weak: the merge option's automatic combination is the one thing this project's non-goal of inferring scientific intent tells us not to build, because a rule that combines by field disjointness will, sooner or later, fuse scientifically entangled edits and report success — a corruption no one is prompted to catch. On *inspectability*, an exported value that is one worker's named revision beats a server-synthesized composite that no worker ever saw whole. Merge wins decisively on *review workload* and *reviewer effort per item* — and those are real, conceded costs, not dismissed ones: coordinators will see more queued items, and each item makes them compare whole visits rather than just the field that diverged. The team weighed that and chose the larger, coarser queue over merge rules it cannot be sure will never combine related edits without noticing. *Implementation/maintenance burden* reinforces the choice: a two-coordinator team with no systems administrator should not be maintaining per-field merge rules.
+
+This turns on the non-goal of inferring scientific intent and the tenets of inspectability and coordinator-operable workflows; it is taken knowingly *against* the pull of minimizing review workload.
+
+**Why the merge approach stays live.** It is not wrong, only outweighed here, and two foreseeable shifts would reopen it. If review volume becomes the binding constraint — coordinators unable to clear the queue before Friday — the automatic clearing of independent edits becomes worth far more than it is today. And if a *narrow, safe* field subset emerges where combination carries no scientific risk (an observer-name typo fix is the standing example), a hybrid that auto-combines only that subset and leaves everything else to whole-visit review could capture much of merge's workload benefit without its central hazard. A future reader hitting either condition should re-run this judgment, not assume it is closed.
+
+**What this obligates downstream (cascade, not co-ownership):**
+
+- **tablet-sync** must retain, per local draft, the `baseRevisionId` it descends from; submit complete visit payloads under an idempotency key; and surface the *unresolved* outcome so a worker learns their submitted visit became a competing revision awaiting coordinator review — worded so it never reads as the edit being lost.
+- **review-export** must provide a revision-comparison screen that shows competing whole revisions side by side; record a resolution as coordinator identity + contributing revision IDs + outcome; and hold unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV until resolved.
+
+Both nodes link back to the record-store contract rather than restating these semantics.
